DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.inffus.2026.104221
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1566253526001004
KW  - Backdoor attacks
KW  - Large language models
KW  - Model security
KW  - Multi-task learning
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success across diverse natural language processing (NLP) tasks such as generation, summarization, and translation. However, their increasing deployment also exposes them to covert security threats, among which backdoor attacks pose particularly severe risks. Existing attacks typically pursue high adversarial effectiveness at the expense of clean-task performance, leaving the underlying trade-off insufficiently understood and poorly controlled. In this work, we revisit this fundamental tension and present a principled optimization framework that reformulates backdoor injection as a bi-objective learning problem balancing malicious and benign objectives. Unlike prior heuristic multi-task formulations, our approach introduces a conflict-aware gradient coordination mechanism and a theoretically grounded parameter selection strategy that jointly ensure efficient, targeted adaptation without full-model tuning. We further provide theoretical guarantees showing that resolving inter-objective gradient conflicts leads to provable convergence benefits and mitigates clean-task degradation. Extensive experiments on multiple LLMs-based architectures and datasets demonstrate that our method consistently achieves strong attack success rates while preserving high clean accuracy and computational efficiency. These results establish a rigorous foundation for analyzing and designing optimization-based backdoor attacks in LLMs.
ER  - 

