AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in cross-domain applications, yet they exhibit significant sensitivity to minor prompt-level perturbations. While adversarial research has emerged to probe these vulnerabilities, existing studies primarily focus on single-domain or discrete token manipulations, which often fail to capture generalized weaknesses across diverse contexts. In this paper, we propose Universal Adversarial Suffix (UAS), a framework that generates input-agnostic perturbations by aggregating data across multiple domains. Our approach identifies transferable semantic and syntactic weaknesses under a unified cross-domain instruction-tuning setting by optimizing a continuous perturbation vector in the embedding space. This vector is then projected back to discrete tokens and appended to prompts. Extensive experiments on scientific, financial, open-domain, and biomedical benchmarks demonstrate that UAS possesses strong cross-domain transferability and consistently degrades model performance. More importantly, we identify a “critical transition phenomenon”: prompt-level perturbations can shift from enhancing performance to causing catastrophic failure. These findings provide new insights into adversarial robustness in cross-domain structured prediction settings for large language models.
