AB  - Low-bit quantization is a practical solution for deploying large Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) language models under strict efficiency constraints, but its impact on model safety remains underexplored. In this work, we study the safety implications of MoE quantization and show that existing quantization methods can substantially increase Attack Success Rate (ASR), weakening the ability of MoE models to provide safe responses to harmful instructions. Through empirical analysis, we find that quantization induces significant routing shifts relative to full-precision models. To interpret these shifts, we introduce Contrastive Safe Expert Specification, which identifies safety-critical experts and reveals two harmful trends: decreased routing frequency of safety-critical experts and increased routing frequency of weakly safety-related experts. Motivated by these findings, we propose Security-Aware Mixture-of-Experts Post-Training Quantization (SAMEQuant), a new quantization framework that preserves safety at both the router and expert levels. Specifically, SAMEQuant preserves safe routing behavior through safety-aware routing alignment with the full-precision router, and restores expert safety capability through safe routing-aware transformation matrix optimization. Extensive experiments on three MoE models demonstrate that SAMEQuant consistently outperforms strong state-of-the-art quantization baselines in preserving safety under quantization.
