AB  - The Model Context Protocol (MCP), which standardizes the interaction of large language models (LLMs) with external tools and data sources, expands the capabilities of artificial intelligence systems while also creating a new and complex attack surface. Due to tool calls, contextual data flow, and multi-step processing chains in the MCP architecture, content-based attacks such as prompt injection, SQL injection, and path traversal, as well as time-based behavioral attacks, can occur. However, existing security approaches mostly focus on content-based analysis and fall short in jointly evaluating real-time, behavioral, and multi-step attack patterns. This experimental study proposes a multi-layered security architecture that provides real-time threat monitoring and risk assessment for MCP-based systems. The proposed approach offers an integrated framework that combines rule-based pre-filtering, LLM-supported semantic analysis, behavioral and temporal anomaly scoring, and deep learning-based attack prediction. In this context, a hybrid model called Sentinel-Net has been developed, combining parallel Dilated 1D CNN, Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) attention mechanism, and LSTM layers. To address the data scarcity specific to MCP, a synthetic data generation method based on Poisson processes, Markov chains, Zipf, and Beta distributions has also been designed. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed CNN–LSTM-based architecture achieves higher performance compared to models such as 1D CNN, Random Forest and Transformer Encoder, which are commonly used and preferred in the literature for cyber attack detection, with an accuracy of 96.07% and an F1 score of 96.52%. This approach offers a low-latency, scalable, and explainable security solution for MCP-based artificial intelligence systems.
