AB  - The rapid proliferation of fake news and social media-based cyberbullying poses significant risks to public trust, democratic discourse, and individual well-being. Although traditionally studied as separate phenomena, growing evidence indicates that misinformation and abusive language frequently co-occur within adversarial online ecosystems, amplifying societal harm and complicating moderation efforts. Existing surveys largely address fake news or cyberbullying in isolation, with limited consideration of their conceptual overlap, multimodal manifestations, or the emerging role of large language models (LLMs) in detection and mitigation. This systematic review synthesizes 112 peer-reviewed studies published between 2018 and 2025, covering machine learning, deep learning, transformer-based architectures, and recent LLM-driven approaches for harmful content detection across diverse datasets, languages, and modalities. Beyond summarizing prior work, the review identifies recurring methodological patterns, evaluation limitations, and persistent challenges, including dataset bias, weak multilingual generalization, limited explainability, and vulnerability to evolving adversarial strategies. Building on evidence extracted from the surveyed literature, we introduce the Joint Harm Detection and Detoxification (JoHaD) framework as an analytically derived reference architecture that unifies fake news and cyberbullying detection while extending moderation toward proactive language detoxification. By consolidating emerging practices such as multimodal signal alignment, joint harm modeling, and verification-oriented rewriting, JoHaD provides a structured foundation for integrated and socially responsible governance of online harm. Overall, this survey offers a comprehensive synthesis of current advances and outlines key directions for developing transparent, multimodal, and ethically aligned artificial intelligence-based moderation systems in the LLM era.
