AB  - Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) typically employ single-pass vision encoders that compress images into static representations during reasoning. This paradigm restricts representational flexibility and prevents visual perception from dynamically aligning with the reasoning path. By contrast, humans solving multimodal problems often perform multi-step reasoning and dynamically attend to the most relevant visual evidence at each step. Motivated by this, we propose Dynamic Visual Re-reading (DVR), a framework that enables adaptive, question-conditioned visual re-encoding during reasoning. DVR decomposes a complex query into a sequence of sub-questions and explicitly signals when to revisit the image via trigger sentences in its reasoning trace. When triggered, the semantic context of the sub-question is injected into the vision encoder, enabling cross-modal interactions that progressively guide the model to focus on relevant visual evidence. Through stepwise question decomposition and iterative visual re-encoding, DVR supports structured reasoning over complex queries while dynamically adjusting visual attention throughout the reasoning process. To effectively couple reasoning decomposition with visual perception, we introduce a two-path training strategy that combines a standard branch with a re-encoding branch supervised by sub-answers. Experiments on five benchmarks show consistent improvements over baselines across different model scales and structures. Qualitative attention visualizations further reveal sub-question-aligned shifts in visual focus, highlighting the effectiveness of dynamic visual re-reading for structured multimodal reasoning.
