AB  - The migration of large language models (LLMs) in the healthcare sector as the disruptive technology has revolutionized the sphere with the advanced natural language comprehension, clinical decision support, and data processing. Their integration in terms of the medical diagnostics, communication with the patient, and research is shown to have an incomparable potential to promote the accuracy, cost-efficiency, and accessibility. The chapter presents the targeted discussion of the applications, issues, and the role of the LLM implementation in the healthcare systems in line with the current tendencies in the area of diagnostics, computerized pathology, medical education, patient safety, and telehealth. The most important findings are that the use of LLMs can assist in the clinical process, enhance the initial diagnosis, enhance the interaction between the patients and the professionals, and help in the personalized care. Nevertheless, the issues that remain (hallucinations, the threat to data privacy, interpretability problems, and regulatory ambiguities and uncertainty) need mitigation procedures that are designed and structures of accountable rollout. The analytical comparison of the recent empirical studies is combined with the systematic literature review to make up methodology. Another finding is the role of the expertise supervision, fine-tuning of domains, and multimodal integration in improving the work of LLMs. It is discussed regarding the future views, moral issues, and technical considerations that are required to offer safe scaling. Altogether, the present research suggests the transformational worth of the LLMs and the necessity to provide active human control, appropriate management, and simple assessment to implement the latter in the modern healthcare environment that will make the process fair, safe, and medically sound.
