AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success across a wide range of tasks, yet they continue to demonstrate vulnerabilities to jailbreak attacks. Existing token-level jailbreak attacks, notably the Greedy Coordinate Gradient (GCG) method, have proven effective in automatically generating adversarial suffixes that compromise even robustly aligned LLMs. However, current token-level jailbreak strategies suffer from several limitations, including a tendency for the optimization process to get stuck in local minima, slow convergence, and insufficient utilization of internal model information in the attack mechanism. To address these limitations, we propose a Trajectory-Aware Dynamic Suffix Optimization with Buffer (TADSOB) framework to optimize jailbreak suffixes, offering a new way for automated red teaming and enhancing the understanding of jailbreak mechanisms. First, TADSOB incorporates dynamic suffix initialization, which refines the precision of gradient-index-guided token replacement in the sampling phase and improves overall computational efficiency. Second, TADSOB extracts magnitude and angular features from the hidden layers of the target model for suffix components and proposes trajectory-guided evaluation metrics to manipulate the model’s reasoning pathways, thereby facilitating LLM jailbreaking. Finally, TADSOB maintains a buffer container storing the top-b suffixes with minimal loss for multi-coordinate updates, accelerating loss convergence. Experimental results show that TADSOB achieves state-of-the-art performance on the AdvBench benchmark across multiple target LLMs, achieving up to a 5.8× speedup and an Attack Success Rate (ASR) of 82% on Llama-2.
