DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nexres.2026.102565
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S3050475926012546
KW  - Fast healthcare interoperability resources (FHIR)
KW  - Large language models (LLMS)
KW  - Model context protocol (MCP)
KW  - Clinical summarization
KW  - Electronic health records (EHR)
AB  - Objective
To develop and evaluate a provenance-bounded architecture for generating narrative patient summaries from Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR) bundles while reducing hallucinations, medication distortion, and unsupported temporal inference. Unconstrained large language models applied directly to fragmented FHIR bundles have been reported to fabricate diagnoses, invent medication changes that were never documented, and infer disease progression not supported by the underlying records; the architecture evaluated here was designed specifically to prevent this class of failure by separating deterministic data preparation from generation. This separation was informed by prior work on FHIR structural mapping and terminology interoperability and on tool-grounded LLM orchestration for auditable clinical decision support.
Methods
We implemented an end-to-end summarization pipeline in which a large language model (LLM) was restricted to validated context produced through deterministic preprocessing, including patient-specific FHIR (Release 4, R4) retrieval, terminology normalization against SNOMED CT, ICD, and LOINC, medication reconciliation, and structured context packaging. The orchestration layer was implemented using the Model Context Protocol (MCP) to expose auditable FHIR-aware tools, each invoked by a single LLM backend (Llama 3.2 3B Instruct, quantized to 4-bit precision, deployed locally through Ollama; exact decoding parameters are reported in Section 4.8). The architecture was evaluated as a proof-of-concept, safety-oriented study using 10 clinician-curated synthetic FHIR bundles representing Parkinson's disease cases with multimorbidity and polypharmacy. Three neurologists with subspecialty expertise in movement disorders and post-residency clinical experience ranging from several years to approximately two decades (further detailed in Section 4.3, including disclosure of the two reviewers who also participated in bundle curation) independently performed blinded assessments across six validation scenarios, scoring diagnostic accuracy, completeness, medication fidelity, longitudinal coherence, and hallucination presence (the first four dimensions rated on a five-point Likert scale), the last operationally defined as any narrative statement that could not be traced to, or was inconsistent with, the source FHIR bundle.
Results
Mean evaluation scores were 4.6/5 for diagnostic accuracy, 4.5/5 for completeness, 4.8/5 for medication fidelity, and 4.6/5 for longitudinal coherence. No hallucinated diagnoses, medications, laboratory values, or temporal distortions were identified in the reviewed summaries. Inter-rater agreement was strong overall (Fleiss' kappa 0.82; 95% CI 0.70–0.91) and observed agreement on hallucination absence was 100% (kappa not estimable because all ratings were identical; see Section 4.6). Stable performance was observed across scenarios involving polypharmacy, sparse laboratory data, acute events, deep brain stimulation, and cross-domain multimorbidity.
Conclusion
In this proof-of-concept architectural evaluation, hallucination-resistant clinical summarization was achievable when deterministic semantic harmonization preceded generation and the LLM operated only on provenance-linked validated context. The findings support MCP-orchestrated, tool-grounded summarization as a promising design pattern for safer FHIR-based clinical decision support. However, broader validation using real-world multi-site datasets and direct comparisons with unconstrained and retrieval-augmented baselines remains necessary, and a protocol for executing that comparison, sharing the same dataset, physician panel, and scoring instrument as the present evaluation, is specified in Section 4.4.
