KW  - Large language model
KW  - Deep safety alignment
KW  - Adaptive curriculum learning
KW  - Adaptive constraints
AB  - Most existing safety alignment methods for large language models (LLMs) constrain only the initial tokens of generation. However, safety signals decay with generation depth and these methods lack adaptivity in capturing deep-level unsafe signals. To tackle these issues, we propose Dual-Side Adaptive Deep Safety Alignment (DADSA), a deep safety alignment framework that adaptively models both generation depth and constraint strength. First, we extend existing safety benchmarks by constructing SafetyPre and HarmSafetyPre, comprising 510 instructions and 510 instruction–response pairs across 17 harmful categories, respectively, to support adaptive deep alignment. Furthermore, we develop a dual-track data regime that balances safety and utility. Second, we propose Adaptive Curriculum Safety Augmentation (ACSA), which combines adaptive risk-aware depth sampling based on token level refusal probabilities with a curriculum depth scheduler, gradually guiding the model from shallow refusal patterns to robust defenses over long contexts and harmful semantic regions. Third, we present Adaptive-γ Soft-Constrained Fine-Tuning (Aγ-SFT), which adaptively adjusts token-level constraint strength as a function of generation position and safety deviation, yielding an optimization paradigm that is stable while preserving shallow safety and enforcing strong deep level refusal. Extensive experiments on mainstream LLMs show that ACSA lowers attack success rate by 21.86% under GCG attacks, and Aγ-SFT lowers attack success rate by 51.27% under fine-tuning attacks.
