AB  - Safety guardrails are essential for the reliable deployment of generative artificial intelligence (AI) systems. However, existing approaches are difficult to scale beyond dominant cultural contexts and may fail to reflect context-specific linguistic patterns, cultural norms, and safety requirements in underrepresented settings. We propose a context-centered framework for decentralized, bottom-up guardrail optimization in specific linguistic and cultural settings. Using Taiwan as a case study, we develop TWGuard, a guardrail optimized for the Taiwan context. TWGuard improves the F1-score by 0.289 over its foundation model and outperforms competing baselines while maintaining competitive performance on a general safety benchmark. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of the framework in the case study and suggest its potential applicability to other low-resource and underrepresented linguistic settings.
